 The main idea is to restrict ourselves to only use the main thread and never use other threads,
 which will stop the need for shared buffers, thus removing the need of cross-origin headers.
 
-To achieve this, simply configure `default_dart_async: false` (which makes every `pub fn` automatically `#[frb(sync)] pub fn`), and then use `pub fn / pub async fn` as usual.
+To achieve this:
+
+1. Disable the `thread-pool` feature of `flutter_rust_bridge` in your `Cargo.toml`, e.g. `flutter_rust_bridge = { version = "...", default-features = false, features = ["anyhow", "dart-opaque", "log", "portable-atomic", "rust-async", "user-utils", "wasm-start"] }`.
+2. Configure `default_dart_async: false` (which makes every `pub fn` automatically `#[frb(sync)] pub fn`), and then use `pub fn / pub async fn` as usual. A function that would still need the thread pool (e.g. `#[frb(dart_async)]` on a non-async `fn`) is reported as a compile error.
+3. Build with `flutter_rust_bridge_codegen build-web --no-threads`. This skips the threaded rustflags and `-Z build-std`, so a stable Rust toolchain is enough and the WASM memory is not shared.
 
 Note that Rust's async system, such as `pub async fn` and [async spawns](../../guides/cross-platform/async), still works well here, because it runs on the main thread by default on the Web platform (though it is multi-threaded on native platforms).
 
